WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.870
Film Courage: Do you think that teaching screenwriting
or these books or whatever is selling false

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:05.870
hope?

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:10.275
This has been one criticism of what we’ve
seen [comments in videos and our channel]

00:00:10.275 --> 00:00:12.080
and I don’t see why this would be selling
false hope?

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:15.000
If [writing] is something someone enjoys and
they love the process of creating?

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.540
Al Watt, Author, Screenwriter and Founder
of L.A. Writers' Lab: Okay I love this!

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:27.760
I believe that my job as a writing teacher
is to facilitate writers in finding the story

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:32.820
that lives within them and stay out of the
result.

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:36.760
In other words when you get into the result
that’s when you get tripped up.

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:39.770
That’s when you don’t really write from
your heart.

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:42.690
That’s when you don’t write something
that’s actually going to be meaningful to

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:47.970
other people and so I always think of writing
as a two-tiered process.

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:54.370
The first tier is you have to write if for
yourself and then the second tier is once

00:00:54.370 --> 00:00:58.190
you’ve written it for yourself, once you’ve
written that stuff where you’re like “Oh

00:00:58.190 --> 00:00:59.700
my gosh, I can’t show this to anybody.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:01.860
It’s way too personal.”

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:07.000
Even if it’s fiction, especially if it’s
fiction, you’ve got to have some skin in

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:08.000
the game.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:11.210
You’ve got to be invested in this thing
that you are writing.

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:16.430
You’ve got to let go of this idea that “Is
it going to sell?”

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:22.690
You can’t…I remember when I wrote my first
novel.

00:01:22.690 --> 00:01:25.390
I woke up one day and I was like I’ve been
writing for a long time.

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:27.190
I was making a living as a stand-up comic.

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:31.620
But I’d been writing for a long time and
I hadn’t sold anything and I thought well

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:33.360
you know let me do the math here.

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:37.422
Clearly I must be a mediocre writer if I’ve
been writing for this long and I haven’t

00:01:37.422 --> 00:01:38.460
sold anything.

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:45.750
So what I’m going to do is I’m going to
stop writing for the marketplace (whatever

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:48.280
that is) and I’m going to write something
for myself.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:53.470
I’m going to write something that really
means something to me and I wrote a story

00:01:53.470 --> 00:01:55.680
that I wrote really quick.

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:04.190
I wrote a novel called Diamond Dogs and got
it through this lawyer friend of mine and

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:05.320
got it to an agent.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:13.080
She auctioned it a week later for a ridiculous
amount of money and then it becomes a best-seller

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:23.370
and it won a bunch of awards and it was the
first time I realized that don’t know anything.

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:28.370
And when I try and … you know I always say
my idea or my story is never the whole story…my

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:36.960
idea who is going to like this or who is going
to like that is…I can’t control the result.

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:39.290
None of us have any control over the result.

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:45.599
But what we do have control over is understanding
that we’re all unique, we all have something

00:02:45.599 --> 00:02:50.490
valuable to say and I really believe that
as a writer and a writing teacher.

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:52.470
So I want to help writers.

00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:58.780
I can’t predict…some of my students have
gone on to become best-selling authors, show

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:01.290
runners, A-list screenwriters.

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:04.920
I didn’t know who they were going to be.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:13.810
But the ingredient that I think is most valuable
isn’t talent whatever that is.

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:19.580
It’s a willingness to learn, it’s a humility,
it’s a willingness to be curious about the

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:21.920
story that lives within you.

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:27.110
It’s the humility to put it on the page
while staying out of the result.

00:03:27.110 --> 00:03:29.010
That’s really hard for people.

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:32.110
That’s the really challenging test.

00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:37.820
Being a writer is a marathon, it’s not a
sprint.

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:45.520
And if you expect immediate results, you might
not stay in the game long enough to experience

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:46.609
the fruits of your labor.

